      "section": "introduction",
      "text": "Traditional technologies for removing PFASs from contaminated water, such as activated carbon adsorption, ion exchange, and reverse osmosis, are often difficult for reasons of cost- effectiveness, efficiency, and long term viability [ 10 ]. These techniques may also generate sec - ondary waste that requires treatment [ 11 , 12 ]. Despite all efforts, exist - ing technologies generally are unable to completely destroy PFAS, so they end up persevering in the environment while also pointing to a key limitation in our capabilities for successful cleanup of these compounds. Briefly, a renewed focus has emerged for the establishment of sustain - able and financially prudent technologies for controlling PFAS con - taminants in aquatic environments. In the past years, the application of biopolymeric materials for the elimination of contaminants has received growing interest because of their renewable nature, environmental benignity, and exceptional effi - ciency in the adsorption of contaminants. Materials like cellulose, algi - nate, and chitosan are especially notable in the area of wastewater treatment because they are biodegradable, nontoxic, and easily modi - fied for the purpose of specially targeting a diverse variety of contami - nants such as heavy metals and organic chemicals. Chitosan, derived from crustacean shells, is especially advantageous due to its high adsorption capacity, ease of processing, and its adaptability in forming composites with various other substances."
